===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{term-label|en|linguistics}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[study#Noun|study]] of the [[way#Noun|way]] [[sound#Noun|sounds]] [[function#Verb|function]] in [[language]]s, including [[accent#Noun|accent]], [[intonation]], [[phoneme]]s, [[stress#Noun|stress]], and [[syllable#Noun|syllable]] [[structure#Noun|structure]], and which sounds are [[distinctive#Adjective|distinctive]] [[unit#Noun|units]] within a language; {{lb|en|countable}} the way sounds function within a [[given#Adjective|given]] language; a [[phonological]] [[system]]. {{defdate|from late 18th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|orthoepy|soundlore<q:dated>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Edmund Fry|title=Prospectus of a New Work, Entitled Pantographia; {{noitalic|[...]}}|location=[London]|publisher=&lbrack;{{glossary|s.n.}}&rbrack;|month=August|year=1798|section=title page|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_prospectus-of-a-new-work_fry-edmund_1798/page/n2/mode/1up|oclc=1326077085|passage=Prospectus of a new work, entitled {{w|Pantographia}}: Containing accurate copies of all the known alphabets in the world. Together with an English explanation of the peculiar force of each letter: To which will be added specimens of all well-authenticated oral languages, Forming a comprehensive Digest of '''phonology'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Notes of the Month|editor=Sylvanus Urban [pseudonym]|journal=[[w:The Gentleman's Magazine|The Gentleman’s Magazine and Historical Review]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Bowyer Nichols|John Bowyer Nichols and Sons]]|month=March|year=1854|volume=XLI (New Series)|page=281|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/gentlemansmagaz42unkngoog/page/281/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=1113090696|passage=The advantages of such a system [of a universal alphabet], both scientific and practical, were urged, the former in connection with the study of ethnology and philology, and the latter chiefly in connection with the great Protestant missionary enterprises of the present time. Professor [[w:Karl Richard Lepsius|[Karl Richard] Lepsius]] and Dr. {{w|Max Müller}} have devoted much time to the subject, founding their '''phonology''' on the physiological principles ably expounded by Dr. Johannes Müller, and published in the Transactions of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:James Richardson Logan|J[ames] R[ichardson] Logan]]|title=The Maruwi of the Baniak Islands|editor=J. R. Logan|journal=The Journal of the Indian Archipelago and Eastern Asia|location=Singapore|publisher={{...|Printed}} [F]or the editor by Jacob Baptist|year=1856|volume=XLI (New Series)|issue=1|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eXMEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA16|oclc=908314851|passage=The Achean, the ancient Malayu and other mixed '''phonologies''' possessing a considerable degree of harshness, were thus formed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Robert Holford Macdowall Bosanquet|R[obert] H[olford] M[acdowall] Bosanquet]]|title=XXXV. On the Present State of Experimental Acoustics, with Suggestions for the Arrangement of an Acoustic Laboratory, and a Sketch of Research.|editors=[[w:Robert Kane (chemist)|Robert Kane]]; William Thomson; William Francis|journal=[[w:Philosophical Magazine|The London, Edinburgh, and Dublin Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Taylor & Francis|Taylor and Francis]],{{nb...|Red Lion Court, Fleet Street}}; sold by [[w:Longman|Longmans, Green, Reader, and Dyer]];{{nb...|Kent & Co.; Simpkin, Marshall and Co.; and Whittaker and Co.;—and by Adam and Charles Black, and Thomas Clark, Edinburgh; Smith and Son, Glasgow;—Hodges, Foster, and Co., Dublin;—Putnam, New York;—and Asher and Co., Berlin.}}|month=October|year=1879|volume=VIII (Fifth Series)|issue=49|page=301|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/s05philosophicalmag08londuoft/page/301/mode/1up|oclc=32062646|passage=The most interesting applications of the phonograph, however, are to the analysis of speech. {{...}} [T]he point in which the proposed arrangements will be of most value is in the analysis of the inflections of speech, or the rapid variations of pitch which occur continually. This analysis is of the highest importance for '''phonology''', as the inflections are undoubtedly among the principal characteristics of dialects.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Nikolaus Ritt|chapter=Mutation, Variation and Selection in Phonological Evolution: A Sketch Based on the Case of Late Middle English a > au/_l{C/#}|editor=Jacek Fisiak|title=Studies in Middle English Linguistics|series=Trends in Linguistics: Studies and Monographs|seriesvolume=103|location=Berlin&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:De Gruyter|Mouton de Gruyter]]|year=1997|page=545|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/studiesinmiddlee0000unse_s7h9/page/545/mode/1up|isbn=978-3-11-015242-5|passage=Crucially, the neat separateness of '''phonologies''' which my account seems to imply is an abstraction and does not mean that the '''phonologies''' represented different regional or social dialects.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Wallace Chafe|Wallace L. Chafe]]|chapter=The Ordering of Phonological Rules|editor=Charles W. Kreidler|title=Phonology: Critical Concepts|location=London&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2000|volume=IV (From Rules to Constraints)|page=219|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5_a3j_SAC7EC&pg=PA219|isbn=978-0-415-20348-7|passage=Thus, underlying 'agtus' was converted first into 'āgtus' by the vowel lengthening rule, and then into 'āktus' by the ancient persistent rule. This example has previously been interpreted as indicating that new rules can enter a '''phonology''' elsewhere than at depth I.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|uncountable}} The study of the way [[component#Noun|components]] of [[sign#Noun|signs]] function in a [[sign language]], and which components are distinctive units within the language; {{lb|en|countable}} the way components of signs function within a given sign language.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David F. Armstrong; [[w:William Stokoe|William C[larence] Stokoe]]; Sherman E. Wilcox|chapter=The Universe of Gesture|title=Gesture and the Nature of Language|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=1995|year_published=1996|page=21|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=GBQwhFJiu_YC&pg=PA21|isbn=978-0-521-46213-6|passage=Sign language linguists have produced many volumes of description of the '''phonology''' and morphology of signs and the syntax of sign languages.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Rachel Sutton-Spence; w:Bencie Woll|chapter=The Structure of Gestures and Signs|title=The Linguistics of British Sign Language: An Introduction|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=1999|year_published=2003|page=154|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lUsCbazTICIC&pg=PA154|isbn=978-0-521-63142-6|passage=The term ‘'''phonology'''’ may seem odd in the context of sign linguistics, since the word has as its root ''phon''&nbsp;– the Greek word for ‘sound’. {{...}} However, sign linguists now prefer the term '''phonology''' to emphasise that the same level of structure exists in sign language and spoken language, despite the differences in modality. The study of sign '''phonology''' began with the work of {{w|William Stokoe}}, the American founder of sign linguistics.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gerald P. Berent|chapter=Sign Language–Spoken Language Bilingualism and the Derivation of Bimodally Mixed Sentences|editors=Tej K. Bhatia; William C. Ritchie|title=The Handbook of Bilingualism and Multilingualism|series=Blackwell Handbooks in Linguistics|edition=2nd|location=Chichester, West Sussex|publisher=w:Wiley-Blackwell|year=2014|section=part II (Neurological and Psychological Aspects of Bilingualism and Multilingualism)|page=352|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=b3PYAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA352|isbn=978-1-4443-3490-6|passage=[A]s with spoken languages, sign language '''phonologies''' are built from a repertoire of distinctive features that are assembled following principled combinatorial constraints. {{...}} ASL [American Sign Language] linguistic properties / a. A '''phonology''' based on hand shape, orientation of the hands, the location and movement of the hands within the signing space, and contact of the hands with each other and the body.}}

